1.9 Comparing models: bias, overfitting and cross-validation
Since several models can represent the same reality, we need to compare them. In this book the best model is, by default, the one that best represents the reality it is meant to approximate for the purpose at hand, and we measure this with performance metrics computed on data the model has not seen (James et al. 2021, ch. 2). In supervised learning the metrics measure the distance between predictions and observed outcomes (mean absolute error, accuracy, area under the ROC curve…); in unsupervised learning they measure how well structure is captured (silhouette, reconstruction error); in reinforcement learning, the cumulative reward; in generative modelling, the fidelity, diversity and safety of what is generated. Each chapter introduces the metrics it needs. Predictive performance is not the only criterion: we also value models that can be interpreted and used to answer scientific questions (Chapter 12), and a good analysis seeks a balance.

Definition 1.8 (Bias, variance, overfitting) A model has high bias when its assumptions are too rigid to capture the real pattern: it underfits and errs systematically. It has high variance when it is so flexible that it follows the noise of its particular training set: it overfits, with excellent training performance and poor performance on new data. Good models balance the two (James et al. 2021, ch. 2).







Figure 1.9: A chair moulded to fit one child perfectly. It is ideal for this child and uncomfortable, or useless, for any other: a model that overfits is tailored to its training data in the same way.



Think of a chair made to measure for one child (Figure 1.9): it fits every curve of that child, and nobody else. A model that overfits has been shaped around the particular patients it was trained on, including their accidental quirks, and fits new patients badly.

Too simple, just right, too flexible. Thirty training points (dots) come from a smooth curve (dashed) plus noise. The model is a polynomial whose degree you choose: degree 1 is a straight line; high degrees bend many times. Watch the training error and the error on 300 new test points.

viewof deg = Inputs.range([0, 15], {value: 1, step: 1, label: "Polynomial degree"})







ofit = {
  function mulberry32(a) { return function() { a |= 0; a = a + 0x6D2B79F5 | 0; let t = Math.imul(a ^ a &gt;&gt;&gt; 15, 1 | a); t = t + Math.imul(t ^ t &gt;&gt;&gt; 7, 61 | t) ^ t; return ((t ^ t &gt;&gt;&gt; 14) &gt;&gt;&gt; 0) / 4294967296; }; }
  const rnd = mulberry32(42);
  const gauss = () =&gt; { let u = 0, v = 0; while (u === 0) u = rnd(); v = rnd(); return Math.sqrt(-2 * Math.log(u)) * Math.cos(2 * Math.PI * v); };
  const truth = x =&gt; Math.sin(3 * x) + 0.3 * x;
  const sample = m =&gt; d3.range(m).map(() =&gt; { const x = rnd() * 2 - 1; return {x, y: truth(x) + 0.35 * gauss()}; });
  const train = sample(30), test = sample(300);
  const cheb = (x, d) =&gt; { const T = [1, x]; for (let k = 2; k &lt;= d; k++) T.push(2 * x * T[k - 1] - T[k - 2]); return T.slice(0, d + 1); };
  function fit(d) {
    const m = d + 1, A = d3.range(m).map(() =&gt; new Array(m).fill(0)), bv = new Array(m).fill(0);
    for (const p of train) { const t = cheb(p.x, d); for (let i = 0; i &lt; m; i++) { bv[i] += t[i] * p.y; for (let j = 0; j &lt; m; j++) A[i][j] += t[i] * t[j]; } }
    for (let i = 0; i &lt; m; i++) A[i][i] += 1e-9;
    for (let c = 0; c &lt; m; c++) { let piv = c; for (let r = c + 1; r &lt; m; r++) if (Math.abs(A[r][c]) &gt; Math.abs(A[piv][c])) piv = r;
      [A[c], A[piv]] = [A[piv], A[c]]; [bv[c], bv[piv]] = [bv[piv], bv[c]];
      for (let r = 0; r &lt; m; r++) if (r !== c) { const f = A[r][c] / A[c][c]; for (let k = c; k &lt; m; k++) A[r][k] -= f * A[c][k]; bv[r] -= f * bv[c]; } }
    const w = bv.map((v, i) =&gt; v / A[i][i]);
    const pred = x =&gt; cheb(x, d).reduce((s, t, i) =&gt; s + t * w[i], 0);
    const rmse = data =&gt; Math.sqrt(d3.mean(data, p =&gt; (p.y - pred(p.x)) ** 2));
    return {pred, train: rmse(train), test: rmse(test)};
  }
  const all = d3.range(0, 16).map(d =&gt; ({d, ...fit(d)}));
  return {train, truth, all};
}







{
  const cur = ofit.all[deg];
  const grid = d3.range(-1, 1.0001, 0.01);
  const verdict = deg &lt;= 2 ? "high bias: too simple to follow the curve" : deg &lt;= 6 ? "a reasonable balance" : "high variance: chasing the noise";
  return html`&lt;p&gt;Degree ${deg}: training error &lt;strong&gt;${cur.train.toFixed(3)}&lt;/strong&gt; · test error &lt;strong&gt;${cur.test.toFixed(3)}&lt;/strong&gt; — ${verdict}.&lt;/p&gt;`;
}







Plot.plot({
  height: 260, grid: true, y: {domain: [-2.5, 2.5], label: "y"}, x: {label: "x"},
  marks: [
    Plot.line(d3.range(-1, 1.0001, 0.01).map(x =&gt; [x, ofit.truth(x)]), {strokeDasharray: "5,4"}),
    Plot.line(d3.range(-1, 1.0001, 0.005).map(x =&gt; [x, Math.max(-2.5, Math.min(2.5, ofit.all[deg].pred(x)))]), {stroke: "#1f6f8b", strokeWidth: 2.5}),
    Plot.dot(ofit.train, {x: "x", y: "y", r: 3, fill: "currentColor"})
  ]
})







Plot.plot({
  height: 200, grid: true, x: {label: "Polynomial degree"}, y: {label: "Error (RMSE)", domain: [0, 1.2]},
  color: {legend: true, domain: ["training", "test"], range: ["#888", "#1f6f8b"]},
  marks: [
    Plot.line(ofit.all, {x: "d", y: d =&gt; Math.min(d.train, 1.2), stroke: () =&gt; "training", strokeWidth: 2}),
    Plot.line(ofit.all, {x: "d", y: d =&gt; Math.min(d.test, 1.2), stroke: () =&gt; "test", strokeWidth: 2}),
    Plot.ruleX([deg], {strokeDasharray: "3,3"})
  ]
})







The training error always falls as the model becomes more flexible; the test error falls and then rises again. The degree with the lowest test error is the one to choose. But we used the test set to make that choice — so it is no longer an honest judge of the chosen model. The standard remedy is cross-validation.

Definition 1.9 (Cross-validation) In \(V\)-fold cross-validation the training data are split into \(V\) parts (folds). Each fold in turn is held out while the model is fitted on the other \(V-1\) folds and evaluated on the held-out one; the \(V\) errors are averaged. Choices such as the polynomial degree are made with the cross-validated error, and the test set is used only once, at the end. With \(V = n\) it is called leave-one-out cross-validation (Stone 1974; James et al. 2021, ch. 5).



  Five-fold cross-validation
  Five rows, one per round. In each row the training data are drawn as five blocks; one different block per row is held out for validation and the other four are used for fitting.

  Round 1Round 2Round 3Round 4Round 5








  held out

Five-fold cross-validation: each fold (highlighted) is held out once while the model is fitted on the other four.

Bias, overfitting and cross-validation all address one question: is the model learning signal or memorising noise? Answering it honestly is what makes a model fit to inform real decisions. Chapter 3 turns these ideas into a working routine.

Your first honest evaluation. Use the book’s fixed split of Ward 7 into a training set (1 000 patients, 80 %) and a test set (250 patients, 20 %), then evaluate the simplest possible model: predict, for every test patient, the average change in creatinine of the training patients. Fill in the blank with the function that computes an average. The last line computes the mean absolute error on the test set.

RPython

















The function that computes the arithmetic average in R is mean().


prediction &lt;- mean(train$delta_creatinine_48h)


















pandas columns have a .mean() method.


prediction = train["delta_creatinine_48h"].mean()




R and Python read the same split, so both give 0.236 mg/dL. This is the first row of the comparison table that Chapter 3 starts and every later chapter extends: each new model of Ward 7 is judged on these same 250 test patients.

3.5 Cross-validation, step by step
Chapter 1 defined \(V\)-fold cross-validation (Definition 1.9); here is the procedure in full (Stone 1974; James et al. 2021, ch. 5). Take the training set, already divided into \(V\) folds of roughly equal size (on Ward 7, \(V = 5\) folds of 200). Then, for each fold \(v = 1, \dots, V\):

set fold \(v\) aside as the validation fold;
fit the model on the other \(V - 1\) folds, including every data-dependent step (choosing variables, standardising, imputing; see Section 3.6);
predict the outcomes of the validation fold and compute the metric, \(\text{MAE}_v\).

Finally, average the \(V\) results:
\[
\text{CV}_{(V)} = \frac{1}{V}\sum_{v=1}^{V} \text{MAE}_v .
\qquad(3.6)\]
Every training patient is used for validation exactly once and for fitting \(V - 1\) times. The spread of the \(V\) fold errors tells you how much the estimate would move with a different split; its standard error, the standard deviation of the fold errors divided by \(\sqrt{V}\), is a rough measure of that uncertainty (Hastie, Tibshirani, and Friedman 2009, ch. 7).

Watch cross-validation happen. Each dot is one of the 1 000 Ward 7 training patients. In each round one fold (coloured) is held out, the model is fitted on the other four (grey) and its MAE is computed on the held-out fold. Round 6 combines the five results. Choose a model, then press Play or move the slider. Notice that the test set never takes part.

icu3 = FileAttachment("data/icu_aki.csv").csv({typed: true})
split3 = FileAttachment("data/splits/ward7_split.csv").csv({typed: true})
w7 = icu3.map((d, i) =&gt; ({...d, set: split3[i].set, fold: split3[i].fold,
                          sx: d.sofa + (((i * 7919) % 101) / 101 - 0.5) * 0.6}))
w7train = w7.filter(d =&gt; d.set === "train")
viewof cvModel = Inputs.radio(["Average model", "Straight line in SOFA"], {value: "Average model", label: "Model"})
viewof cvPlay = Inputs.toggle({label: "Play", value: false})
viewof cvManual = Inputs.range([1, 6], {value: 1, step: 1, label: "Round (6 = combine)"})



















































cvRound = {
  if (!cvPlay) { yield cvManual; return; }
  let r = 0;
  while (true) { yield (r % 6) + 1; r += 1; await Promises.delay(1400); }
}
cvFits = {
  const fitFn = rows =&gt; {
    const ys = rows.map(d =&gt; d.delta_creatinine_48h), my = d3.mean(ys);
    if (cvModel === "Average model") return {a: my, b: 0};
    const xs = rows.map(d =&gt; d.sofa), mx = d3.mean(xs);
    const b = d3.sum(xs, (x, i) =&gt; (x - mx) * (ys[i] - my)) / d3.sum(xs, x =&gt; (x - mx) ** 2);
    return {a: my - b * mx, b};
  };
  return [1, 2, 3, 4, 5].map(v =&gt; {
    const f = fitFn(w7train.filter(d =&gt; d.fold !== v));
    const val = w7train.filter(d =&gt; d.fold === v);
    return {fold: v, a: f.a, b: f.b, mae: d3.mean(val, d =&gt; Math.abs(d.delta_creatinine_48h - (f.a + f.b * d.sofa)))};
  });
}
{
  const r = cvRound;
  const box = (label, sub, on, dashed) =&gt; html`&lt;div style="flex:1; min-width:58px; padding:6px 4px; text-align:center; border-radius:6px; font-size:0.82rem; border:1.5px ${dashed ? "dashed" : "solid"} ${on ? "var(--aihs-accent)" : "var(--aihs-border)"}; background:${on ? "var(--aihs-accent-soft)" : "transparent"}"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;${label}&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;${sub}&lt;/div&gt;`;
  const cells = cvFits.map(f =&gt; box(`Fold ${f.fold}`,
    r === 6 ? `MAE ${f.mae.toFixed(3)}` : (r === f.fold ? "validation" : "fitting"), r === f.fold || r === 6, false));
  const cv = d3.mean(cvFits, f =&gt; f.mae), se = d3.deviation(cvFits, f =&gt; f.mae) / Math.sqrt(5);
  if (r === 6 && window.aihs) window.aihs.complete("ch03-tryit-cv");
  return html`&lt;div style="display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:6px; margin:0.4rem 0"&gt;${cells}${box("Test set", "250 · locked", false, true)}&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;${r &lt; 6
    ? html`Round ${r}: fitted on folds ${[1, 2, 3, 4, 5].filter(v =&gt; v !== r).join(", ")}; MAE on fold ${r} = &lt;strong&gt;${cvFits[r - 1].mae.toFixed(3)}&lt;/strong&gt; mg/dL.`
    : html`Cross-validated MAE = average of the five = &lt;strong&gt;${cv.toFixed(3)}&lt;/strong&gt; mg/dL (standard error ${se.toFixed(3)}).`}&lt;/p&gt;`;
}























{
  const r = cvRound, lines = r === 6 ? cvFits : [cvFits[r - 1]];
  return Plot.plot({
    height: 290, grid: true, color: {type: "identity"},
    x: {label: "SOFA score on admission", domain: [-0.5, 18.5]},
    y: {label: "Change in creatinine over 48 h (mg/dL)"},
    marks: [
      Plot.dot(w7train, {x: "sx", y: "delta_creatinine_48h", r: 1.8,
        fill: d =&gt; (r === 6 || d.fold === r) ? "#1f6f8b" : "#999",
        fillOpacity: d =&gt; (r === 6 || d.fold === r) ? 0.6 : 0.25}),
      ...lines.map(f =&gt; Plot.line([[0, f.a], [18, f.a + 18 * f.b]], {stroke: "#b35c00", strokeWidth: r === 6 ? 1.5 : 3}))
    ]
  });
}







Plot.plot({
  height: 170, grid: true, marginLeft: 50,
  x: {label: "Validation fold", domain: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5], type: "band"},
  y: {label: "MAE (mg/dL)", domain: [0, 0.3]},
  marks: [
    Plot.barY(cvFits.filter(f =&gt; cvRound === 6 || f.fold &lt;= cvRound), {x: "fold", y: "mae", fill: "#1f6f8b", fillOpacity: 0.7}),
    cvRound === 6 ? Plot.ruleY([d3.mean(cvFits, f =&gt; f.mae)], {stroke: "#b35c00", strokeWidth: 2}) : null,
    Plot.ruleY([0])
  ]
})








The five fold errors are not identical; they differ by up to a few hundredths of a mg/dL, because some folds happen to contain easier patients than others. That variation is exactly why a single split can mislead, and why averaging over folds gives a steadier estimate.
How many folds? With larger \(V\) each fit uses more of the data, so the estimate is closer to the error of a model trained on all of it, but the fits overlap more, the \(V\) errors are more alike, and the computation grows. Leave-one-out cross-validation (\(V = n\)) is the extreme: almost unbiased, but variable and expensive for slow models. Five or ten folds are the usual compromise (James et al. 2021, ch. 5; Hastie, Tibshirani, and Friedman 2009, ch. 7). Two refinements are common. Repeated cross-validation reruns the whole procedure with several random fold assignments and averages them, which steadies the estimate further. Stratified folds keep the proportion of an outcome (say, AKI yes/no) similar in every fold (Kuhn and Johnson 2013, ch. 4).
Folds must respect the structure of the data. If one patient contributes several rows, as a HomeBeat participant does with one row per day, all of that person’s rows must sit in the same fold; otherwise the model is partly validated on the very people it was fitted on. The same logic, applied to hospitals, gives leave-one-site-out cross-validation: fit on three Ward 7 hospitals, test on the fourth, and rotate. It is the internal-external validation of Section 3.2, and we use it at the end of this chapter.

With five-fold cross-validation on the 1 000 Ward 7 training patients, how many times is the model fitted, and on how many patients each time?

Once, on 1 000 patients That is a single fit on the whole training set: it gives a model but no honest error estimate.
Five times, on 800 patients each time Correct. Each round leaves out one fold of 200 and fits on the remaining 800. (After cross-validation has guided the choices, the chosen model is usually refitted once more on all 1 000.)
Five times, on 200 patients each time The other way round: each fit uses four folds (800 patients) and is validated on the fifth (200).
1 000 times, on 999 patients each time That is leave-one-out cross-validation, the case \(V = n\).

3.7 \(k\)-nearest neighbours: a first learner
So far our models have been averages and straight lines. Here is a different idea, close to how clinicians reason from experience: to predict what will happen to Aoife, look at the past patients who were most like her, and see what happened to them.

Definition 3.5 (\(k\)-nearest neighbours) \(k\)-nearest neighbours (\(k\)NN) predicts the outcome of a new instance \(\mathbf{x}\) by finding the \(k\) training instances closest to it, the set \(N_k(\mathbf{x})\), and averaging their outcomes (for a numerical outcome) or taking a vote (for a category):
\[
\hat y(\mathbf{x}) = \frac{1}{k}\sum_{i \in N_k(\mathbf{x})} y_i .
\qquad(3.7)\]
“Closest” is measured by a distance between feature vectors, usually the Euclidean distance on standardised features (Cover and Hart 1967; James et al. 2021, ch. 2).

\(k\)NN has an unusual property: it has no fitting step. “Training” consists of storing the training patients; all the work happens at prediction time. Two ingredients need care.
Distance. On Ward 7 we use two predictors, the SOFA score and baseline creatinine, and measure how far apart two patients are with the ordinary straight-line (Euclidean) distance in the plane of the two. Chapter 10 is devoted to distances and to what happens to them when there are many predictors; for now the plane is enough.
Scale. SOFA runs from 0 to 18 points; baseline creatinine from about 0.4 to 3 mg/dL. Measured raw, one SOFA point would count as much as a whole mg/dL of creatinine, purely because of the units. So each predictor is standardised: the training mean is subtracted and the result divided by the training standard deviation, putting both on a comparable scale. With standardisation the predictions do not depend on the units. Without it they do: if creatinine is recorded in µmol/L (1 mg/dL = 88.4 µmol/L), its large numbers dominate the distance and the cross-validated MAE of unstandardised \(k\)NN worsens from 0.181 to 0.189 mg/dL, while standardised \(k\)NN gives 0.179 in either unit. Note the word training: the means and standard deviations are learned from the training rows only (from the fitting folds during cross-validation), or the scaling itself leaks (Section 3.6).
The number of neighbours \(k\) controls flexibility. With \(k = 1\) every prediction copies a single past patient: the model follows every accident of the data, the high-variance extreme of Definition 1.8. Its training error is close to zero, because each training patient is (nearly) its own nearest neighbour; on Ward 7 it is 0.072 mg/dL. Its cross-validated error is 0.254, worse than the average model. As \(k\) grows the predictions are averaged over more patients and become smoother; with \(k\) equal to the whole training set every prediction is the training mean, the high-bias extreme. Somewhere in between lies the best compromise, and only data the model has not seen can tell us where.

Patients like Aoife. The background shows the \(k\)NN prediction of the 48-hour creatinine change for every combination of SOFA and baseline creatinine; the grey dots are the 1 000 training patients and the three labelled points are tonight’s patients. The circled dots are Aoife’s \(k\) nearest neighbours. The lower chart shows the training error and the five-fold cross-validated error for every \(k\). Move \(k\) and watch the map change from patchy to smooth. Target: find a \(k\) whose cross-validated MAE is within 0.001 mg/dL of the smallest possible.

knnCurve = FileAttachment("data/precomputed/ch03/knn_curve.csv").csv({typed: true})
knnSummary = FileAttachment("data/precomputed/ch03/summary.json").json()
tonight3 = FileAttachment("data/ward7_tonight.csv").csv({typed: true})
viewof kNN = Inputs.range([1, 150], {value: 1, step: 1, label: "Number of neighbours k"})






























knnPrep = {
  const tr = w7train, K = 150;
  const xs = tr.map(d =&gt; d.sofa), cs = tr.map(d =&gt; d.baseline_creat), ys = tr.map(d =&gt; d.delta_creatinine_48h);
  const mx = d3.mean(xs), sx = d3.deviation(xs), mc = d3.mean(cs), sc = d3.deviation(cs);
  const zx = xs.map(x =&gt; (x - mx) / sx), zc = cs.map(c =&gt; (c - mc) / sc);
  const neigh = (s, c) =&gt; {
    const a = (s - mx) / sx, b = (c - mc) / sc;
    const d = zx.map((z, i) =&gt; ({i, d: (z - a) ** 2 + (zc[i] - b) ** 2}));
    d.sort((p, q) =&gt; p.d - q.d || p.i - q.i);
    const cum = new Float64Array(K); let acc = 0;
    for (let j = 0; j &lt; K; j++) { acc += ys[d[j].i]; cum[j] = acc; }
    return {cum, idx: d.slice(0, K).map(e =&gt; e.i)};
  };
  const c0 = 0.4, c1 = 2.6, ny = 28, cs0 = (c1 - c0) / ny, grid = [];
  for (let s = 0; s &lt;= 18; s++) for (let b = 0; b &lt; ny; b++) {
    const c = c0 + (b + 0.5) * cs0;
    grid.push({x1: s - 0.5, x2: s + 0.5, y1: c0 + b * cs0, y2: c0 + (b + 1) * cs0, cum: neigh(s, c).cum});
  }
  const pts = tonight3.map(p =&gt; ({name: p.name.split(" ")[0], sofa: p.sofa, creat: p.baseline_creat, ...neigh(p.sofa, p.baseline_creat)}));
  const jit = tr.map((d, i) =&gt; ({sx: d.sofa + (((i * 7919) % 101) / 101 - 0.5) * 0.6, c: d.baseline_creat}));
  return {grid, pts, jit};
}







{
  const row = knnCurve[kNN - 1], best = d3.min(knnCurve, d =&gt; d.cv_mae);
  const hit = row.cv_mae &lt;= best + 0.001;
  if (hit && window.aihs) window.aihs.complete("ch03-tryit-knn");
  const p = knnPrep.pts.map(q =&gt; `${q.name} ${(q.cum[kNN - 1] / kNN).toFixed(2)}`).join(" · ");
  return html`&lt;p&gt;k = &lt;strong&gt;${kNN}&lt;/strong&gt;: training MAE &lt;strong&gt;${row.train_mae.toFixed(3)}&lt;/strong&gt; ·
  cross-validated MAE &lt;strong&gt;${row.cv_mae.toFixed(3)}&lt;/strong&gt; mg/dL
  (average model ${knnSummary.ward7.baselines[0].cv_mae.toFixed(3)}).&lt;br&gt;
  Predicted change tonight: ${p} mg/dL
  ${hit ? html`&lt;br&gt;✓ This k is in the flat bottom of the cross-validation curve.` : ""}&lt;/p&gt;`;
}







Plot.plot({
  height: 330, marginLeft: 50,
  x: {label: "SOFA score on admission", domain: [-0.5, 18.5]},
  y: {label: "Baseline creatinine (mg/dL)", domain: [0.4, 2.6]},
  color: {type: "linear", scheme: "ylorrd", domain: [-0.1, 0.9], clamp: true, legend: true, label: "Predicted change (mg/dL)"},
  marks: [
    Plot.rect(knnPrep.grid, {x1: "x1", x2: "x2", y1: "y1", y2: "y2", fill: d =&gt; d.cum[kNN - 1] / kNN, fillOpacity: 0.85}),
    Plot.dot(knnPrep.jit.filter(d =&gt; d.c &lt;= 2.6), {x: "sx", y: "c", r: 1.2, fill: "#555", fillOpacity: 0.45}),
    Plot.dot(knnPrep.pts[0].idx.slice(0, kNN).map(i =&gt; knnPrep.jit[i]).filter(d =&gt; d.c &lt;= 2.6), {x: "sx", y: "c", r: 4, stroke: "#1f6f8b", strokeWidth: 1.5}),
    Plot.dot(knnPrep.pts, {x: "sofa", y: "creat", r: 6, fill: "white", stroke: "black", strokeWidth: 2}),
    Plot.text(knnPrep.pts, {x: "sofa", y: "creat", text: "name", dy: -13, fontWeight: 700, fill: "black", stroke: "white", strokeWidth: 3, paintOrder: "stroke"})
  ]
})







Plot.plot({
  height: 210, grid: true, marginLeft: 50,
  x: {label: "Number of neighbours k", type: "log", domain: [1, 150]},
  y: {label: "MAE (mg/dL)", domain: [0, 0.27]},
  color: {legend: true, domain: ["training", "cross-validated"], range: ["#888", "#1f6f8b"]},
  marks: [
    Plot.line(knnCurve, {x: "k", y: "train_mae", stroke: () =&gt; "training", strokeWidth: 2}),
    Plot.line(knnCurve, {x: "k", y: "cv_mae", stroke: () =&gt; "cross-validated", strokeWidth: 2.5}),
    Plot.ruleY([knnSummary.ward7.baselines[0].cv_mae], {strokeDasharray: "4,4"}),
    Plot.text([[150, knnSummary.ward7.baselines[0].cv_mae]], {text: ["average model"], textAnchor: "end", dy: -6, fontSize: 11}),
    Plot.text([[1.1, 0.02]], {text: ["← high variance"], textAnchor: "start", fontSize: 11}),
    Plot.text([[150, 0.02]], {text: ["high bias →"], textAnchor: "end", fontSize: 11}),
    Plot.ruleX([kNN], {strokeDasharray: "3,3"})
  ]
})








The cross-validation curve has the shape you saw for polynomial degree in Chapter 1, mirrored: small \(k\) is the flexible end. Its minimum is at \(k\) = 15, with a cross-validated MAE of 0.179 mg/dL, but the curve is almost flat between about 10 and 50 neighbours: any \(k\) in that range would serve equally well.
Now compare with Table 3.3. \(k\)NN clearly beats the average model (0.241) and the SOFA line (0.196), but not the straight line in the same two predictors (0.176). This is a useful surprise. A flexible learner does not automatically beat a simple one: when the true relationship is close to a straight line, as it is in the simulation behind Ward 7, the line uses all 1 000 patients to estimate two slopes, while \(k\)NN uses only a handful of neighbours for each prediction and pays for that flexibility with variance. Only a fair comparison, on the same folds and against a sensible baseline, reveals this.
Why learn \(k\)NN at all, then? Because it is the simplest learner that has a genuine hyperparameter, because it is easy to explain to a patient (“among 15 past patients most like you, the average rise was…”), and because its weaknesses teach a lot. It needs standardised predictors; it becomes slow when the training set is large, since every prediction scans all of it; and with many predictors “nearest” loses its meaning, a problem Chapter 10 examines.

