A \(k\)NN model with \(k = 1\) has a training MAE close to zero on Ward 7. Why is that not good news?

Because a training error of zero means the code has a bug There is no bug: with \(k = 1\) each training patient is (nearly) its own nearest neighbour, so it simply copies its own outcome.
Because the model has memorised the training patients; its error on new patients is much larger Correct. With \(k = 1\) the cross-validated MAE is 0.254, worse even than predicting the average. A near-zero training error is the signature of overfitting, not of skill.
Because \(k = 1\) ignores the predictors It uses them fully, to find the single closest patient. It is the large-\(k\) extreme that ignores them, predicting the overall mean.

