4.5.1 Leverage, outliers and influence
Not all unusual patients are a problem, and the three words used for them mean different things.

Definition 4.6 (Leverage, outlier and Cook’s distance) An instance has high leverage \(h_{ii}\) when its predictor values are far from those of the other instances: it could pull the line strongly. An outlier is an instance with a large residual: the model fits it badly. An instance is influential when removing it would change the fitted coefficients noticeably. Cook’s distance \(D_i\) combines the two ingredients, \[
D_i = \frac{e_i^2}{(p+1)\,s^2}\cdot\frac{h_{ii}}{(1-h_{ii})^2},
\] where \(s^2\) is the residual variance and \(p+1\) the number of coefficients (Cook 1977).

A patient with high leverage who lies on the line is harmless; an outlier with average predictor values moves the line only a little; the combination of the two is what makes a point influential (Belsley, Kuh, and Welsch 1980). Common rules of thumb flag leverage above \(2(p+1)/n\) and Cook’s distance above \(4/n\) for a closer look, and treat \(D_i &gt; 1\) as a serious warning (Fox and Weisberg 2019). They are flags, not verdicts: the right response is to check the record (a typing error in a laboratory value?) and to report whether conclusions change without the patient, not to delete it quietly. On Ward 7, 63 patients pass the \(4/n\) flag, as you would expect by chance in 1 250, and none comes anywhere near 1.
For collinearity, the variance inflation factor of predictor \(j\) is \(\text{VIF}_j = 1/(1-R_j^2)\), where \(R_j^2\) is the \(R^2\) of a regression of \(x_j\) on all the other predictors. It says how many times larger the variance of \(\hat\beta_j\) is than it would be if \(x_j\) were unrelated to the other predictors; values above 5 or 10 are usually taken as a sign of trouble (James et al. 2021, ch. 3). On Ward 7 the largest VIF is 1.71, for sofa: no concern.

