Which statement best contrasts bagging and boosting?

Both grow their trees in sequence; boosting just uses more trees Bagging grows its trees independently (in parallel); only boosting is sequential.
Bagging averages independent deep trees to reduce variance; boosting adds shallow trees in sequence, each fitted to the remaining errors, to reduce bias Correct. Bagging tames unstable, low-bias trees by averaging; boosting builds a flexible model out of weak, high-bias learners by correcting them step by step.
Bagging is for classification, boosting for regression Both work for either kind of outcome.
Boosting cannot overfit because each tree is small It can: with enough rounds and a large learning rate boosting fits the training data almost perfectly and gets worse on new data, as the XGBoost curves below show.

7.2 The artificial neuron
In 1943 Warren McCulloch and Walter Pitts proposed a mathematical neuron that adds up binary inputs and fires when the sum crosses a threshold (McCulloch and Pitts 1943). In 1958 Frank Rosenblatt added what was missing: weights that are learned from examples. He called his machine the perceptron (Rosenblatt 1958).

Definition 7.1 (Artificial neuron (perceptron)) An artificial neuron receives numerical inputs \(x_1, \dots, x_p\), multiplies each by a weight \(w_j\), adds a bias \(b\), and passes the weighted sum \[
z = w_1 x_1 + w_2 x_2 + \cdots + w_p x_p + b = \mathbf{w}^{\top}\mathbf{x} + b
\qquad(7.1)\] through an activation function \(g\), giving its output \(\hat y = g(z)\). The weights and the bias are the neuron’s parameters: they are learned from data. Rosenblatt’s original perceptron used a step activation.

The weights say how much each input matters, and in which direction: a large positive weight pushes the output up, a negative one pushes it down, a weight near zero means the input is ignored. The bias shifts the point at which the neuron “fires”, whatever the inputs; it plays a role loosely similar to the firing threshold of a nerve cell.

Definition 7.2 (Activation function) An activation function \(g\) turns the weighted sum \(z\) into the neuron’s output. Common choices are the step function, \(g(z) = 1\) if \(z \ge 0\) and \(0\) otherwise (Rosenblatt’s perceptron); the logistic (sigmoid) function, \(g(z) = \sigma(z) = 1/(1+e^{-z})\), which gives a number between 0 and 1; and the rectified linear unit (ReLU), \(g(z) = \max(0, z)\), which passes positive values unchanged and sets negative ones to zero and is the default inside modern networks (Nair and Hinton 2010).

