7.3 How a network learns: loss and gradient descent
Every method in this book runs the same learning loop: guess with the current settings, mark the guess by measuring how wrong it is, adjust the settings to make it less wrong, and repeat (Definition 1.6). You ran it by hand with the sliders above. A computer runs it with two ingredients: a loss function to do the marking and gradient descent to do the adjusting.
Marking. For a yes/no outcome the usual loss is the log-loss, also called binary cross-entropy: \[
\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{w}, b) = -\frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \Big[ y_i \log \hat p_i + (1 - y_i) \log (1 - \hat p_i) \Big],
\qquad(7.2)\] where \(\hat p_i\) is the neuron’s output for patient \(i\). A confident correct prediction costs almost nothing; a confident wrong one (\(\hat p_i\) near 0 for a patient who did develop AKI) costs a great deal. Minimising Equation 7.2 is the same as maximising the likelihood of Chapter 5. For a numerical outcome the usual loss is the mean squared error.
Adjusting. The loss depends on the weights. Imagine it as a landscape: each position is a choice of weights, and the height is the loss. We want the lowest valley. Standing somewhere on the slope, the gradient — the vector of partial derivatives \(\partial \mathcal{L} / \partial w_j\) — points uphill, in the direction of steepest increase. So we take a small step the other way.

Definition 7.3 (Gradient descent) Gradient descent minimises a loss by repeating the update \[
w_j \leftarrow w_j - \eta \, \frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial w_j} \quad \text{for every weight } w_j \text{ (and likewise for the bias)},
\qquad(7.3)\] where the learning rate \(\eta &gt; 0\) sets the size of each step. Too small a learning rate makes progress painfully slow; too large a one overshoots the valley and can bounce around or diverge (Goodfellow, Bengio, and Courville 2016).

For a single sigmoid neuron with the log-loss the gradient has a remarkably simple form: \[
\frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial w_j} = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} (\hat p_i - y_i)\, x_{ij},
\qquad
\frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial b} = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} (\hat p_i - y_i).
\] Each patient contributes their error \((\hat p_i - y_i)\) times their input. If the neuron under-predicts for patients with high lactate, the gradient for the lactate weight is negative, and the update raises that weight. This is the computational analogue of synaptic plasticity: connections that would have helped get stronger.

Gradient-descent stepper. To keep the picture one-dimensional, the model here has one weight: \(\hat p = \sigma(b + w \times \text{SOFA})\), with SOFA standardised and the bias \(b\) fixed at its best value. The curve is the log-loss for every possible \(w\); the dots are the steps taken from the start, \(w = -1\). Increase the number of steps, then change the learning rate. Target: reach the bottom (within 0.01 of the best \(w\)) in at most 10 steps. Then try a learning rate above 15.

gd = {
  const ms = d3.mean(icu7, d =&gt; d.sofa), ss = d3.deviation(icu7, d =&gt; d.sofa);
  const xs = icu7.map(d =&gt; (d.sofa - ms) / ss);
  const ys = icu7.map(d =&gt; d.delta_creatinine_48h &gt;= 0.3 ? 1 : 0);
  const n = xs.length, sig = z =&gt; 1 / (1 + Math.exp(-z));
  let b = 0, w = 0;
  for (let it = 0; it &lt; 50; it++) {            // Newton's method for the best (b, w)
    let g0 = 0, g1 = 0, h00 = 0, h01 = 0, h11 = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) { const p = sig(b + w * xs[i]), e = p - ys[i], v = p * (1 - p);
      g0 += e; g1 += e * xs[i]; h00 += v; h01 += v * xs[i]; h11 += v * xs[i] * xs[i]; }
    const det = h00 * h11 - h01 * h01;
    b -= (h11 * g0 - h01 * g1) / det; w -= (h00 * g1 - h01 * g0) / det;
  }
  const loss = ww =&gt; { let s = 0; for (let i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) { const p = Math.min(1 - 1e-12, Math.max(1e-12, sig(b + ww * xs[i])));
    s -= ys[i] * Math.log(p) + (1 - ys[i]) * Math.log(1 - p); } return s / n; };
  const grad = ww =&gt; { let s = 0; for (let i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) s += (sig(b + ww * xs[i]) - ys[i]) * xs[i]; return s / n; };
  return {b, wStar: w, loss, grad, curve: d3.range(-3, 5.001, 0.04).map(ww =&gt; ({w: ww, L: loss(ww)}))};
}
