Uncertainty for neural networks and other black boxes



The convolutional network of Chapter 7 gives each chest X-ray a single probability. Exact Bayesian inference for millions of weights is out of reach, but practical approximations exist: keeping dropout switched on at prediction time and repeating the prediction many times (Gal and Ghahramani 2016), or training several networks from different random starts and looking at how much they disagree (deep ensembles) (Lakshminarayanan, Pritzel, and Blundell 2017). Either way, the spread of the predictions plays the role of the posterior spread here, and can be used in the same way: to refer the most uncertain cases to a radiologist (Kompa, Snoek, and Beam 2021). Chapters 12 and 18 return to this use of uncertainty, for explanations and for language models.



