On a computer, the same agent trained with \(\epsilon = 0.2\) and \(\eta = 0.1\) chooses a near-optimal action in 18 or more of the 20 states after about 75 episodes, and in 20 of 20 after 3 000 (the Lab reproduces this). It discovered the anticipating policy of Table 14.3 without ever seeing Table 14.2 — but only because it could try every action in every state of a simulator, thousands of times, at no cost to anyone. A real patient offers neither. With \(\epsilon = 0\), the agent stops trying actions whose first outcome was disappointing, and some states never get a fair test: the exploration problem of the bandit again, now spread over twenty states.
Real problems have far more states than twenty — Aoife’s state could include dozens of continuous measurements — and a table cannot hold them. Deep reinforcement learning replaces the table with a neural network (Chapter 7) that takes the state as input and outputs \(\hat Q(s, a)\) for each action; trained with the same target, it learned to play dozens of video games from the pixels (Mnih et al. 2015). The ideas of this section carry over unchanged; so do the difficulties of the next one, magnified.

In Equation 14.5, why does the target use \(\max_{a'} \hat Q(s', a')\) rather than the value of the action the agent will actually take next?

Because the agent always takes the best action next Not when it explores: with ε-greedy choice it sometimes takes a random action. Q-learning’s target ignores that on purpose.
So that the table learns the value of acting greedily, even while the agent explores Correct. This makes Q-learning off-policy: the behaviour can be exploratory while the estimates describe the greedy policy. A method that used the next action actually taken (called SARSA) would learn the value of the exploratory policy instead.
Because the maximum is easier to compute than an average Both are trivial to compute for three actions. The choice changes which policy’s value is learned.
To make the estimates as optimistic as possible The max is not about optimism; it reflects the Bellman optimality equation. (In noisy problems it can in fact make estimates slightly too high, a known side effect.)



