With the original window of five words and all three refinements on, NegEx-lite gets 86 % of the sentences right; with none of the refinements, only 60 %. The best any setting achieves is 94.6 %. The sentences that still fail at the best settings are instructive: “rash-free since stopping the antibiotic” (no trigger word at all), “fever: none”, “itching resolved within a day” (the finding was real but is no longer present), and “cannot rule out a drug rash”, where a trigger expresses uncertainty, not negation. Rules capture the common patterns cheaply and transparently; the long tail of human phrasing escapes them. That long tail is the argument for learning from data, which the rest of this chapter starts to make.

With the termination rule switched off, NegEx-lite says that in “No fever but a new rash on the trunk” the rash is negated. Why?

Because “rash” is not in the list of findings The finding is whatever you ask about; the program found rash without difficulty.
Because rash is five words after the trigger “no”, just inside the five-word window, and nothing stops the scope at “but” Correct. Without a termination rule the scope of “no” runs straight through “but”. Termination words mark where one clause ends and another, with a different subject, begins.
Because “no fever but” is a pseudo-negation Pseudo-negations are phrases such as “no increase” or “not only”, which contain a trigger but do not negate. “No fever” is a genuine negation, of fever.

15.6 Subwords: byte-pair encoding and its relatives
Between words and characters lies the compromise used by almost every modern language model: subword tokens. Frequent words stay whole; rare words are built from frequent pieces. A model that has never seen immunotherapies but knows immun, o and therapies can still represent it, and what it learns about therapy is shared by chemotherapy and radiotherapy.

Definition 15.5 (Byte-pair encoding (BPE)) Byte-pair encoding builds a subword vocabulary from a corpus. Start with every word split into characters, plus an end-of-word marker. Count every pair of adjacent symbols, weighting each word by its frequency; merge the most frequent pair into a new symbol; repeat for a chosen number of merges. The list of merges, applied in order, then tokenises any new word. BPE began as a data-compression method (Gage 1994) and was adapted to split rare words in neural machine translation (Sennrich, Haddow, and Birch 2016).

The explorer below runs BPE on a tiny corpus of twenty clinical words, each with a frequency. Before any merge, every word is a row of single letters followed by the end-of-word marker _. The first merges join the most common pairs: y + _, then h + e, then r + a. Then, step by step, the pieces of therapy assemble, because therapy, chemotherapy, immunotherapy, radiotherapy and therapies together make it the most frequent string in the corpus.

Tokeniser explorer. Move the slider to apply more merges and watch the corpus words collapse into subwords. Type any new word to see how the current merges split it. The default, chemotherapies, is not in the corpus. Target: find a number of merges at which a word that is not in the corpus is split into three tokens or fewer.

bpeCorpus = [["therapy", 12], ["therapies", 4], ["chemotherapy", 10], ["immunotherapy", 6], ["radiotherapy", 5],
  ["toxic", 3], ["toxicity", 8], ["nephrotoxic", 4], ["nephrotoxicity", 5], ["hepatotoxicity", 3],
  ["cardiotoxicity", 2], ["neutropenia", 6], ["thrombocytopenia", 3], ["nausea", 9], ["vomiting", 6],
  ["reaction", 7], ["reactions", 4], ["related", 8], ["unrelated", 4], ["rash", 7]]
bpeApply = (sy, a, b) =&gt; { const out = []; let i = 0;
  while (i &lt; sy.length) { if (i &lt; sy.length - 1 && sy[i] === a && sy[i + 1] === b) { out.push(a + b); i += 2; } else { out.push(sy[i]); i++; } }
  return out; }
bpeMerges = {
  let words = bpeCorpus.map(([w, c]) =&gt; ({w, c, sy: [...w, "_"]}));
  const merges = [];
  for (let step = 1; step &lt;= 100; step++) {
    const counts = new Map();
    for (const x of words) for (let i = 0; i &lt; x.sy.length - 1; i++) {
      const k = x.sy[i] + "\u0001" + x.sy[i + 1]; counts.set(k, (counts.get(k) || 0) + x.c); }
    if (counts.size === 0) break;
    let best = null, bc = -1, ba = "", bb = "";
    for (const [k, c] of counts) { const [a, b] = k.split("\u0001");
      if (c &gt; bc || (c === bc && (a &lt; ba || (a === ba && b &lt; bb)))) { best = k; bc = c; ba = a; bb = b; } }
    merges.push({step, a: ba, b: bb, count: bc});
    words = words.map(x =&gt; ({...x, sy: bpeApply(x.sy, ba, bb)}));
  }
  return merges;
}
viewof bpeK = Inputs.range([0, bpeMerges.length], {value: 0, step: 1, label: "Number of merges"})
viewof bpeWord = Inputs.text({label: "New word to split", value: "chemotherapies"})
bpeSeg = (word, k) =&gt; { let sy = [...String(word).toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z]/g, ""), "_"];
  for (const m of bpeMerges.slice(0, k)) sy = bpeApply(sy, m.a, m.b); return sy; }
