Two lessons come out of a few minutes with the search box. First, TF-IDF matters: with raw counts, words such as with, on and the dominate the vectors, and long notes win simply by being long and repetitive. Second, retrieval inherits the blindness of the bag of words. Search “gentamicin hearing” across all documents and the top hits are discharge summaries that say “no ringing in the ears or hearing change reported”: the words match; the meaning is the opposite of what the family wanted. A search engine retrieves candidates; a person, or a later step that reads more carefully, must judge them. Exactly this pattern returns in Chapter 18, where TF-IDF-like retrieval becomes the “R” of retrieval-augmented generation (Lewis et al. 2020), the step that fetches documents for a language model to read before it answers.

