Figure 16.2: From scores to probabilities. The model scores every token of the vocabulary; softmax exponentiates the scores and divides by their sum, so that they become positive and add up to one.



How should the model then pick the next token? The choice is called decoding, and it has a large effect on what the text looks like.

Definition 16.7 (Decoding: greedy, sampling, temperature, top-k, top-p) Greedy decoding always takes the most probable token: it is deterministic and tends to repeat itself. Sampling draws the next token at random in proportion to its probability, so the same prompt can give different texts. Two knobs shape the distribution before sampling. The temperature \(\tau &gt; 0\) divides the logits before the softmax, \[
P_\tau(w_j) = \frac{e^{z_j / \tau}}{\sum_k e^{z_k / \tau}} ,
\qquad(16.5)\] so that \(\tau &lt; 1\) sharpens the distribution (as \(\tau \to 0\) sampling becomes greedy) and \(\tau &gt; 1\) flattens it (as \(\tau \to \infty\) every token becomes equally likely). Top-\(k\) sampling keeps only the \(k\) most probable tokens and renormalises (Fan, Lewis, and Dauphin 2018); top-\(p\) (nucleus) sampling keeps the smallest set of most probable tokens whose probabilities add up to at least \(p\) (Holtzman et al. 2020).

With the logits above, \(\tau = 0.5\) gives probabilities 0.942, 0.047 and 0.012; \(\tau = 2\) gives 0.554, 0.262 and 0.184. The order never changes; only how decisively the model commits to it.

Turn the temperature knob. The distributions below come from the small neural language model of Section 16.8, trained on the Ward 7 notes: for each context it produced one logit for each of the 131 tokens. Choose a context, then move the temperature and top-\(k\) sliders and draw samples. The effective number of choices is \(2^H\), the perplexity of the distribution itself. Task: after “patient reports”, find a setting where the model is choosing among fewer than two effective options, and then one where it is choosing among more than twenty.

