Try the context “the surgeon says”. At \(\tau = 1\) the model gives he a probability of 87 % and she 13 %, almost exactly the shares in the training notes, where 87 % of surgeons are followed by he. Lower the temperature and she disappears altogether: a greedy autocomplete always writes he, an exaggeration of a skew that was already in the data. That is what happened on Seán’s screen.
Two lessons for clinical use follow. First, generation involves chance: unless decoding is greedy, the same prompt can produce different notes, so a system’s output on one occasion is a sample, not the answer. Second, a fluent sample is not a checked fact. The model writes what is probable given its training text, and the probable and the true can part company; text that is fluent, confident and wrong is called a hallucination (Ji et al. 2023), and Chapter 18 examines how to detect and limit it. For drafting clinical documentation, low temperatures are usually preferred because consistency matters more than variety.

A hospital configures its note-drafting assistant with a temperature close to 0. Which consequence should the clinical safety officer expect?

The same prompt will almost always give the same draft, and the most common phrasing in the training notes will dominate Correct. Near \(\tau = 0\) decoding is greedy: reproducible, but it always picks the majority continuation, exaggerating any skew in the training text (as with he after “the surgeon says”).
The drafts will be more accurate, because the model is more confident Temperature changes how decisively the model commits to its own ranking; it does not make the ranking more correct. A confident wrong token stays wrong.
The drafts will be more varied and creative That is what a high temperature does: it flattens the distribution and lets unlikely tokens through.
The model will refuse to answer when it is uncertain Nothing in decoding abstains: softmax always produces a distribution, and decoding always picks a token.

17.4 Masks: who may look at whom
So far every token could attend to every other, including the ones after it. For a model that reads a whole note and classifies it, that is exactly what we want. For a model that generates text one token at a time, it is cheating. The chain rule in Equation 17.1 asks for the probability of \(w_t\) given the tokens before it. If, during training, position \(t\) could look at position \(t+1\), the model would simply copy the answer it is supposed to predict, and it would learn nothing it could use when the future is genuinely unknown.
The fix does not change the attention computation; it changes the scores before the softmax.

Definition 17.3 (Causal mask) A causal mask stops each position from attending to later positions. A mask matrix \(\mathbf{M}\) with \(M_{ts} = 0\) if \(s \le t\) and \(M_{ts} = -\infty\) if \(s &gt; t\) is added to the scores, \[
\operatorname{softmax}\!\left( \frac{\mathbf{Q}\mathbf{K}^{\top}}{\sqrt{d_k}} + \mathbf{M} \right) \mathbf{V} .
\qquad(17.4)\] Because \(e^{-\infty} = 0\), every blocked position receives a weight of exactly zero, and the remaining weights in each row still add up to 1. The allowed positions form a lower triangle: token \(t\) sees itself and everything before it, never anything after (Vaswani et al. 2017; Radford et al. 2018).

Other masks use the same trick. A padding mask blocks the filler tokens added to make notes of different lengths fit in one table, so that no real word attends to them. A model reading a whole note uses no causal mask at all: it is bidirectional, and “pain” can attend to “denies” before it and to “today” after it.

Switch the mask on and off. The table shows the attention weights for “the patient reports chest pain” (rows: the token being updated; columns: the token looked at), computed from a fixed table of scores. Switch on the causal mask, the one used by text-generating models, and watch the upper triangle drop to zero while every row still adds up to 1.

