18.4 Retrieval-augmented generation
A language model’s knowledge is frozen in its parameters at the end of training, it cannot say where a fact came from, and it knows nothing about Ward 7’s local rules. The most widely used remedy is to look the facts up first and give them to the model together with the question (Lewis et al. 2020).

Definition 18.6 (Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), chunk, retriever, grounding) In retrieval-augmented generation, a system answers a question in three steps: a retriever searches a collection of documents, split into short passages called chunks, and returns the \(k\) chunks most similar to the question; the chunks are inserted into the prompt with instructions to answer from them; the language model generates the answer (Lewis et al. 2020). An answer is grounded when every claim in it is supported by the retrieved passages, ideally with a citation to the passage that supports it.







