Figure 18.3: Retrieval-augmented generation in the Ward 7 guideline assistant.



The retriever is not new to you. In Chapter 15 a document became a TF-IDF vector and two documents were compared with the cosine of the angle between their vectors (Spärck Jones 1972; Salton and Buckley 1988). RAG uses exactly that, with the question playing the role of a very short document. For each of the \(J\) chunks and each word \(w\), the weight is the word’s count in the chunk times its inverse document frequency, \(\mathrm{idf}(w) = \ln\!\big((1 + J)/(1 + \mathrm{df}(w))\big) + 1\), where \(\mathrm{df}(w)\) is the number of chunks that contain \(w\); rare words weigh more. The score of chunk \(j\) for the query is \[
\text{score}(\mathbf{q}, \mathbf{d}_j) = \frac{\mathbf{q} \cdot \mathbf{d}_j}{\lVert \mathbf{q} \rVert \, \lVert \mathbf{d}_j \rVert} ,
\qquad(18.4)\] and if every vector has been scaled to length 1 the score is just the dot product. Production systems often replace TF-IDF vectors by dense embeddings from a neural network (Chapter 16), so that “renal” can match “kidney” (Karpukhin et al. 2020), or combine the two; the logic of the pipeline is the same.
The Ward 7 assistant searches a small collection of 31 chunks from 11 local documents: the AKI bundle, the vancomycin and gentamicin guidelines, the sepsis guideline, a few procedures from the other running examples, and so on. The collection was written for this book to illustrate retrieval; it is not clinical guidance. You can read every chunk in the data file.

Inspect the retriever. Choose a question, or type your own, and see which chunks the TF-IDF retriever returns, with their cosine scores; the words that match the query are highlighted. Below, open the prompt that the system would send to the language model. Target: try at least three questions, including “What is the maximum dose of rentosertib?” (rentosertib is a drug we meet in Section 18.9; it appears nowhere in the guidelines). Look at what the retriever returns.

ragCorpus = FileAttachment("data/ch18_guidelines.csv").csv()
ragIndex = {
  const stop = new Set(["a","an","and","are","as","at","be","by","can","do","does","for","from","has","have",
    "how","i","if","in","is","it","its","not","of","on","or","our","should","so","that","the","their",
    "them","then","there","these","they","this","to","was","we","what","when","which","while","who",
    "will","with","without","you","been","into","than","after","before","more","most","also","all",
    "any","every","each","may","only","other","such","whenever","where","one","two"]);
  const tok = s =&gt; s.toLowerCase().split(/[^a-z0-9]+/).filter(w =&gt; w.length &gt;= 2 && !stop.has(w));
  const docs = ragCorpus.map(d =&gt; tok(d.section + " " + d.text));
  const J = docs.length, df = new Map();
  docs.forEach(ws =&gt; new Set(ws).forEach(w =&gt; df.set(w, (df.get(w) || 0) + 1)));
  const idf = new Map([...df].map(([w, n]) =&gt; [w, Math.log((1 + J) / (1 + n)) + 1]));
  const vec = ws =&gt; {
    const v = new Map();
    ws.forEach(w =&gt; { if (idf.has(w)) v.set(w, (v.get(w) || 0) + 1); });
    let ss = 0;
    for (const [w, c] of v) { const x = c * idf.get(w); v.set(w, x); ss += x * x; }
    const n = Math.sqrt(ss);
    if (n &gt; 0) for (const [w, x] of v) v.set(w, x / n);
    return v;
  };
  return {tok, vecs: docs.map(vec), query: q =&gt; vec(tok(q))};
}
