19.3 Variational autoencoders
A variational autoencoder (VAE) makes two changes to the autoencoder (Kingma and Welling 2014; Rezende, Mohamed, and Wierstra 2014).

The encoder proposes a small cloud, not a point. For each image it outputs a mean \(\mathbf{\mu}\) and a standard deviation \(\mathbf{\sigma}\) for each latent coordinate, and the code is drawn from the normal distribution \(\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{\mu},
\mathbf{\sigma}^2)\). Nearby codes must therefore decode to similar images.
The codes are pulled towards one simple shape. A penalty keeps every image’s cloud close to the standard normal distribution \(\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, \mathbf{I})\) — mean 0, standard deviation 1 in each coordinate. After training, the clouds of all the images together fill that shape, so a code drawn from \(\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, \mathbf{I})\) lands where the decoder knows what to do.








