Why the reverse process takes many small steps (a pretend denoiser)

If we knew the exact noise \(\mathbf{\varepsilon}\) that was added, Equation 19.6 could be solved for the clean image in one go: \(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_0 = (\mathbf{x}_t - \sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_t}\,
\hat{\mathbf{\varepsilon}}) / \sqrt{\bar\alpha_t}\). A trained network only guesses the noise. Below, the guess is a mix of the true noise and an unrelated one; move its quality and the step \(t\) above, and see how quickly a one-jump clean-up falls apart once \(t\) is large — dividing by a small \(\sqrt{\bar\alpha_t}\) magnifies every error. Real diffusion models therefore remove only a little noise per step, and re-estimate it at every step.

